1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,300
What Are Stars Made Of?

2
00:00:02,250 --> 00:00:19,787
Stars are self-gravitating plasma spheres composed primarily of hydrogen (approximately 73% by mass) and helium (approximately 25%), with trace amounts of heavier elements—collectively termed "metals" in astrophysical terminology—comprising the remaining 2%.

3
00:00:19,787 --> 00:00:27,625
Stars form through gravitational collapse of cold molecular clouds (typically 10-20 Kelvin) in the interstellar medium.

4
00:00:27,625 --> 00:00:36,737
As the protostar contracts, gravitational potential energy converts to thermal energy, raising core temperature until hydrogen fusion ignites.

5
00:00:36,737 --> 00:00:40,762
Two primary fusion pathways operate in stellar cores.

6
00:00:40,762 --> 00:01:00,187
In stars of less than approximately 1.5 solar masses, including the Sun, the proton-proton chain dominates: two protons fuse to form deuterium, which captures another proton to form helium-3, and two helium-3 nuclei fuse to produce helium-4, releasing approximately 26.7 MeV per cycle.

7
00:01:00,187 --> 00:01:11,512
In more massive stars, the carbon-nitrogen-oxygen (CNO) cycle—where carbon, nitrogen, and oxygen serve as catalysts—accounts for the majority of energy production.

8
00:01:11,512 --> 00:01:19,612
The Sun's core reaches temperatures of approximately 15.7 million Kelvin and densities 150 times that of water.

9
00:01:19,612 --> 00:01:27,787
The Hertzsprung-Russell diagram classifies stars by spectral type (OBAFGKM, from hottest to coolest) and luminosity class.

10
00:01:27,787 --> 00:01:38,975
O-type stars exceed 30,000 Kelvin and show ionized helium lines; M-type stars are cooler than 3,700 Kelvin and display molecular titanium oxide bands.

11
00:01:38,975 --> 00:01:45,450
Main-sequence stars fuse hydrogen in their cores for approximately 10 billion years for a solar-mass star.

12
00:01:45,450 --> 00:01:52,025
When core hydrogen is exhausted, fusion shifts to a hydrogen-burning shell around an inert helium core.

13
00:01:52,025 --> 00:01:56,587
The outer envelope expands and cools, producing a red giant.

14
00:01:56,587 --> 00:02:05,912
Helium ignition proceeds via the triple-alpha process—three helium-4 nuclei fusing to carbon-12—at approximately 100 million Kelvin.

15
00:02:05,912 --> 00:02:16,975
In stars above approximately 8 solar masses, successive fusion stages produce silicon, sulfur, and finally iron-56, at which point further fusion is endothermic.

16
00:02:16,975 --> 00:02:24,200
The core collapses in under a second, triggering a Type II supernova that enriches the interstellar medium with heavy elements.

17
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:34,700
The B2FH paper (Burbidge, Burbidge, Fowler, and Hoyle, 1957) established the theoretical framework for stellar nucleosynthesis.

18
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:40,625
Every element heavier than helium—except trace amounts from the Big Bang—originated in stars.

19
00:02:40,625 --> 00:02:55,375
Carbon and oxygen in biological molecules were synthesized in red giant and supernova progenitors; iron in hemoglobin was forged in silicon-burning shells of massive stars; gold and uranium were produced in neutron-star mergers detected as kilonovae.

